    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Спасибо, ребята, давайте теперь работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you, guys, let us work now.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'thank you' before landing on 'to work'. Fedya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ладно, поздравляю тебя с победой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, I congratulate you on the victory.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'all right' before landing on 'victory'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ой, мне вдруг стало плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, I suddenly felt sick.' Senya anchors the line on 'oh', while 'badly' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Это у тебя от нервов?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is that from your nerves?' The meaning hinges on 'this' working together with 'nerve' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Это такая честная борьба, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is such a fair fight, right?' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'this' and 'so' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тимур, ты вообще куда смотрел?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Timur, where were you even looking?' Notice how Russian expresses 'Timur' and 'watched' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Кругом одна сплошная коррупция!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'All around there is nothing but corruption!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'all around' and finishing on 'corruption'. Eleonora answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто привёз в отель лошадь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who brought a horse into the hotel?' Eleonora anchors the line on 'who', while 'horse' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Простите, я её крепко привязал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Forgive me, I tied her up tightly.' The grammar packs 'forgive' and 'to tie' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Но она отвязалась и убежала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But she came untied and ran away.' In Russian, the idea of 'but' flows naturally into 'ran away' without extra filler words. Eleonora answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она носится и кусает гостей!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She rushes around and bites the guests!' The meaning hinges on 'she' working together with 'guests' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Больше таких инцидентов не будет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There will be no more such incidents.' The meaning hinges on 'more' working together with 'will be' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
